[38;5;252m[1m## Two findings worth stating[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mNaN payloads are not bit-reproducible, and cannot be.[22m My first run showed mismatches like C [38;5;10m0x7fc00000[0m vs Rust [38;5;10m0x7ff96482[0m. Cause: x86 [38;5;10mmulss[0m/[38;5;10maddss[0m return the [3mdestination[23m operand when either source is NaN, so the payload depends on register allocation, which differs between GCC -O0 and rustc. This only occurs when an [3minput[23m is already NaN — for non-NaN inputs, SSE synthesizes the fixed indefinite [38;5;10m0xffc00000[0m, which both sides produce identically. So the suite asserts strict bit-equality everywhere and switches to NaN-tolerant comparison only for NaN inputs ([38;5;10mt_nan_inputs_agree[0m). This is unobservable through the public [38;5;10maabb()[0m, which returns an int from comparisons. I did not change the C or the Rust for this.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe 20-iteration cap in [38;5;10mc2GJK[0m is unreachable.[22m Across ~144k adversarial cases (magnitudes 1e-6 to 1e35, non-normalized rotors, degenerate shapes) the maximum was 3 iterations. With proxies of at most 4 vertices, the duplicate-support check and the monotone [38;5;10md1 > d0[0m descent test bound the loop well below 20. I assert the other five exits are reached and report this one rather than pretending to cover it.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
As a robustness check beyond what was asked, I also ran the release-profile Rust against C built at [38;5;10m-O1[0m, [38;5;10m-O2[0m, and [38;5;10m-O3[0m. All match, which confirms the bit-exact agreement is a genuine property rather than an artifact of CMake's default unoptimized build.[0m[0m
